  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       3. PLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="cp2000-section" aria-labelledby="explanation-heading">
    <h2 id="explanation-heading">What Is a CP2000 Notice — and Why You Shouldn't Panic</h2>

    <p>
      If you open your mailbox and find an IRS CP2000 letter proposing thousands of dollars in additional tax, your natural reaction might be anxiety or panic. But understanding what a CP2000 notice actually is can help you respond calmly and effectively.
    </p>

    <h3>1. A CP2000 Notice Is NOT a Bill or an Audit</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>CP2000 notice</strong> (also called an Underreporter Inquiry) is simply an automated computer-generated proposal. The IRS matching system compared the 1099s, W-2s, and 1099-Rs sent by banks and employers against what was reported on your Form 1040 and noticed a discrepancy.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Why Over 40% of CP2000 Notices Are Wrong</h3>
    <p>
      CP2000 notices are notoriously prone to false alarms for seniors because IRS computers lack full context. Common reasons for erroneous CP2000 letters include:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Non-Taxable IRA Rollovers (Form 1099-R):</strong> You moved funds from one IRA to another within 60 days. The bank issued a 1099-R, but the IRS computer treated it as taxable income.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing Stock Cost Basis (Form 1099-B):</strong> You sold shares of stock or mutual funds. The broker reported the gross sale price, but omitted what you paid for the shares. The IRS assumes a $0 cost basis.</li>
      <li><strong>Exempt Social Security Benefits (Form SSA-1099):</strong> Social Security benefits were miscalculated or entered on the wrong line of Form 1040.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="cp2000-info-box">
      <p class="cp2000-info-box-heading">The 30-Day Response Deadline Is Crucial</p>
      <p>
        You have <strong>30 calendar days</strong> from the Notice Date printed on the top right of your CP2000 to respond. If you need more time to gather records or talk to a tax professional, call the IRS telephone number listed on page 1 of your CP2000 immediately to request a 30-day extension.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       4. WORKED EXAMPLE (STEP BY STEP)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="cp2000-section cp2000-section--tinted" aria-labelledby="worked-example-heading">
    <h2 id="worked-example-heading">Step-by-Step Worked Example: Maria's $18,000 Rollover Notice</h2>
    <p>
      Here is a real-world example showing how a retiree successfully resolved a CP2000 notice using our response framework.
    </p>

    <div class="cp2000-example-card">
      <div class="cp2000-example-scenario">
        <span class="cp2000-example-label">Scenario</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Maria</strong> received a CP2000 notice proposing that she owed <strong>$4,200 in tax plus $350 in interest</strong> because she failed to report an $18,000 distribution from her 401(k) plan. In reality, Maria executed a direct trustee-to-trustee rollover of the $18,000 directly into a new Traditional IRA.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="cp2000-example-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Check the Notice Date:</strong>
          Maria's CP2000 notice date was October 10. Her 30-day deadline was <strong>November 9</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Gather Proof of Non-Taxable Status:</strong>
          Maria requested Form 5498 from her new IRA custodian, which documented the $18,000 deposit as a tax-free rollover.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Complete CP2000 Response Form:</strong>
          Maria checked <strong>Option 2 (I Disagree)</strong> on the IRS response form.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write Explanation &amp; Mail Package:</strong>
          Maria attached a brief letter stating that the distribution was a direct non-taxable rollover, attached a copy of Form 5498, and sent it via <strong>USPS Certified Mail with Return Receipt</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>IRS Resolution:</strong>
          Four weeks later, the IRS sent Maria a <strong>CP2005 closure letter</strong> confirming that her explanation was accepted and her balance due was <strong>$0</strong>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div style="background: #fff8f0; border-left: 4px solid #E8761A; border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 700; color: #E8761A; margin: 0 0 0.25rem 0;">💡 Pro Tip</p>
        <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">Never send original documents to the IRS — send copies. Always mail your CP2000 response using USPS Certified Mail with Return Receipt. The tracking number is your only legal proof that you met the strict 30-day response deadline if the IRS loses your paperwork.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       COMMON PITFALLS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="cp2000-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Ignoring the CP2000 Notice or Missing the 30-Day Deadline</h3>
    <p>Failing to respond within the 30-day window causes the IRS to issue a formal statutory notice of deficiency (CP3219A), which restricts your options to petitioning the Tax Court.</p>
    <p>The fix is to respond via certified mail within 30 days of the notice date or call the IRS to request an extension.</p>

    <h3>Assuming a 1099-B Stock Sale CP2000 Means You Owe Tax on the Entire Sale Amount</h3>
    <p>If you forget to report a stock sale, the IRS assumes your cost basis was $0, making the entire gross proceeds look like taxable profit.</p>
    <p>The fix is to file a completed Schedule D and Form 8949 with your response to provide proof of your actual cost basis.</p>

    <h3>Paying the Proposed Tax Amount Immediately Without Checking 1099-R Rollovers</h3>
    <p>Direct IRA rollovers are non-taxable, but if the distribution code (like 1 or 7) is misreported or the rollover isn't clearly marked on your tax return, the IRS will propose a tax assessment.</p>
    <p>The fix is to attach Form 5498 showing the rollover deposit to your dispute letter instead of paying the proposed amount.</p>
  </section>

